### 7. No Request Body Size Limit (OWASP A05 – Security Misconfiguration)

**Risk:** No `express.json()` middleware is configured, meaning there is no body parser — and therefore no payload size cap. When one is added later, forgetting the `limit` option opens the server to memory-exhaustion (DoS) via oversized payloads.

**Recommendation:**
```js
// Always set an explicit limit when adding body parsing
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true, limit: '10kb' }));
```

---

### 8. No CORS Policy (OWASP A05 – Security Misconfiguration)

**Risk:** Express's default CORS behavior is restrictive — until someone adds `cors()` without configuration. Establishing an explicit allowlist now prevents a future misconfiguration.

**Recommendation:**
```js
const cors = require('cors'); // npm install cors

const allowedOrigins = (process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS ?? '').split(',');

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      callback(null, true);
    } else {
      callback(new Error('CORS policy violation'));
    }
  },
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
  credentials: true,
}));
```

---

### 9. No Centralized Error Handler (OWASP A09 – Security Logging & Monitoring Failures)

**Risk:** Unhandled errors will bubble up to Express's default error handler, which may leak stack traces, file paths, or internal details to API consumers.

**Recommendation:**
```js
// Add LAST, after all routes
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  const requestId = req.headers['x-request-id'] ?? crypto.randomUUID();
  // Log full detail internally
  console.error({ requestId, error: err.message, stack: err.stack });
  // Return sanitized response externally — never expose stack traces
  res.status(err.status ?? 500).json({
    error: 'An unexpected error occurred',
    requestId, // lets users report issues without exposing internals
  });
});
```

